1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
I can't be so sick of that.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Quiet on the set!

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Quiet, please!

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
First position, please!

5
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Rolling, action!

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Hello everyone and welcome to another edition of Director's Cut.

7
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,000
My name is Marco.

8
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,000
My name is Catherine.

9
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
And today we have a very special edition of Director's Cut, as you know.

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Yesterday was the Oscars, or well, two days ago, I think.

11
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Sunday.

12
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Sunday night, it's always the march?

13
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
March, yeah, I think so.

14
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The 82nd annual Academy Awards was held.

15
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
And so we're going to talk about that today.

16
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
We have the results of all the prizes that were handed out.

17
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Best picture, best actress, best supporting actor, etc.

18
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
So we're going to talk about that.

19
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Yeah, also known as the Oscars.

20
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The Oscars.

21
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
To avoid confusion, the Academy Awards and the Oscars are the same thing.

22
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,000
The Oscars is a nickname, of course.

23
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
And the Academy is the Academy of Motion Pictures and Sciences.

24
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
So this is a group of people that decides all about film and awarding certain people

25
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
for extraordinary achievements in film every year.

26
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,000
That's right.

27
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
So before we get into the big winners, why don't we start off with some of the things

28
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
that we may not know too much about.

29
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
For example, we have best cinematography.

30
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
What is the cinematography of a movie?

31
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Okay, cinematography is very important.

32
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
The cinematographer, the person who does this,

33
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
is basically the person who sits behind the camera

34
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
and who says this is where the camera will go.

35
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
So movies with beautiful cinematography are beautifully shot.

36
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
That means that each frame is like a painting.

37
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And it's very technical.

38
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
You have to know all about math and angles and light.

39
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
You basically choose what the camera will see.

40
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Interesting.

41
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
So best cinematography went to Avatar.

42
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Yeah, this was actually a surprise for many people.

43
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Really?

44
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Yeah, because people thought, well, Avatar would do very well in art direction

45
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
and editing and things like that, technical things.

46
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
But cinematography is generally something that's given to very beautiful,

47
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
luscious historical pieces or beautiful pieces that have shots at the outdoors.

48
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
One example that comes to mind is the English Patient,

49
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
which is a movie about war in Africa and, I think, World War II.

50
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
And this has these gorgeous shots of planes crashing in the desert.

51
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And that, to me, is really great cinematography.

52
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
So to have a movie that's almost all in...

53
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,000
CGI.

54
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Yeah, in front of a green screen, you know, it's not shot outside.

55
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
It was really interesting as a choice.

56
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
I guess also it makes it harder, in a sense, because you have no scenes.

57
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
So you have to imagine what's going to be there.

58
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Yeah.

59
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
So I guess there is a lot of effort that went into it and a lot of...

60
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
It was very well done.

61
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Absolutely, yeah.

62
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
And well, speaking of which, you talked about art direction

63
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:02,000
and actually Avatar took also another award for Best Art Direction.

64
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
What does that mean exactly to art direction?

65
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Well, art direction is basically...

66
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
The art director is in charge of the artistic decisions about color and sets

67
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
and all these kind of different things that go into the ambiance,

68
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
the feelings that a movie gives you.

69
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
And movies that have really great art direction

70
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
tend to have really great colors and combinations of things.

71
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
You know, these people have to work with the costume people.

72
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
They have to work with the sound people.

73
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Art direction is all about controlling the artistry of a film.

74
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Wow, that's really great.

75
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And well, Avatar took that one as well.

76
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Now, moving on, there's this interesting one called Best Original Score.

77
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
So you wouldn't really understand what that means.

78
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
What is a score?

79
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
A score is a piece of music.

80
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
But we generally say a score is all of the parts of a piece of music.

81
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
So what the piano plays, what the guitar plays, what the cello plays.

82
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
So in an orchestra, the director looks at the score.

83
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
The musicians look at their parts.

84
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
So Best Original Score is best overall piece of music.

85
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
It's got music for instruments of all kinds,

86
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
and it's got different singers' parts,

87
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
and so the score is the whole piece of music together.

88
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Okay, and so the Best Original Score,

89
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
which means that it was written specifically for the film.

90
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
That's right, not taken from another thing.

91
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Exactly. What went to Up?

92
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Up.

93
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Yeah, the animated film.

94
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Oh, Up was so cute.

95
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Yeah, actually it was a very good choice

96
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
because the music really gave you a really good idea of what was happening

97
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:37,000
and it really got you into the movie.

98
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
I really like this film, Up.

99
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
All right, so Best Original Score went to Up.

100
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Moving on to something else that's really interesting is screenplay.

101
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
So we have Best Adapted Screenplay.

102
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
What is a screenplay?

103
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Well, screenplay is, if you break it down, a play for the screen.

104
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
That means it is the words the actors speak.

105
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:01,000
It's basically the story.

106
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
But it's more than a story because it's the settings

107
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
and the interactions between the characters.

108
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
So basically, if a movie could be a book first, this is the book.

109
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
And it's what everyone uses to know what to do.

110
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,000
It's a screenplay.

111
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
It's all the things that happen in the movie.

112
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
And so actors must memorize their lines from the screenplay.

113
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Directors, they pick a screenplay to make into a movie.

114
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Right. So basically, in this case, it won.

115
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Precious was a movie that won.

116
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
And it's because it was based on a novel push by Sapphire.

117
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Of course, it's adapted screenplay because you took the book

118
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
and then you adapted it for the movies.

119
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,000
That's right.

120
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
So to adapt means to change in order to fit something new.

121
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
In this case, a book can't be a movie.

122
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
You need to give people lines.

123
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
You need to tell the director or the cameraman what to do.

124
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
And so a book is adapted.

125
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
It's changed to fit a movie.

126
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
And that's an adapted screenplay.

127
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Very good.

128
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
And so the movie Precious took it.

129
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
And now moving on to original screenplay.

130
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
That's a screenplay or a book, so to say.

131
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
But that was written specifically for the movies, right?

132
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
It was never a book. It was never a novel.

133
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
It was always a screenplay.

134
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
So the person who wrote it was a screenwriter.

135
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Okay. A screenwriter. Very good.

136
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
And that one went to the drama The Hurt Locker.

137
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,000
All right.

138
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Well, I'm glad we're starting to talk about The Hurt Locker

139
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
because this was a big movie this year.

140
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Okay.

141
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
So the original screenplay went to Hurt Locker.

142
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
And Best Director also went to Hurt Locker,

143
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
which was, oddly enough, a woman for the first time.

144
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,000
It shouldn't be odd.

145
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Well, no. It's the first time, right?

146
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,000
I think excitingly it was a woman, yes.

147
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Catherine Bigelow has made a number of movies.

148
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
All kinds of movies.

149
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
And I'm really happy that she won because it's the first time a woman has ever won Best Director.

150
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
In like 82 years of the Academy Awards, this is the first time a woman has won.

151
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Yeah. There have been other women who were nominated, but they never won.

152
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,000
And I think this is really important.

153
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
It's inspiring for young women who are trying to be in the film industry

154
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
because still most directors are men.

155
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Right, right, right.

156
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Yeah. I guess if you think about it, like all the names that pop up in your mind,

157
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,000
like Steven Spielberg and James Cameron, they're all guys,

158
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
but very few women who are directors out there.

159
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:20,000
That's right.

160
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
And how amazing that her movie is this gritty, intense war movie.

161
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Yeah, very macho.

162
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,000
Or not even macho, but it's generally a macho topic.

163
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Right, exactly. War, exactly.

164
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
And I think that this is even more interesting as a situation.

165
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
I guess that's part of it.

166
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
I guess that's part of the reason why it was so good.

167
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:49,000
Maybe this whole war theme that's very, as you say, usually very, very related,

168
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,000
very masculine, but it gave it a little bit more of a dramatic touch

169
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:59,000
and a more inspirational touch that I guess women can do very well in this case.

170
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Well, and I appreciate about this movie that it doesn't say,

171
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
hey, war is bad and this is why.

172
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Right, right, right.

173
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
It's thoughtful and it's difficult and you have a hard time watching it

174
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
because these characters are just, they're living such dangerous lives,

175
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
but it's not the same as the typical war movie where it's like...

176
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
All shooting and dying.

177
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,000
Yeah.

178
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
All right, so that's the best director.

179
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:30,000
And moving on, before we get to best picture and best actresses,

180
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
let's go to best supporting actress and best supporting actor.

181
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
All right, so supporting actress and actor, what does that mean?

182
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
So basically in movies you have many different characters

183
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
and there's an obvious main character, usually a man,

184
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
and an obvious main character who's a woman.

185
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
But sometimes you have other characters who are very important,

186
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
they're not the most important.

187
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
So, for example, in Charlie Brown, Charlie Brown is the main character,

188
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,000
but Snoopy is a very important character, so he would be the supporting actor.

189
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:08,000
But in movies like, for example, in a movie like Inglorious Basterds,

190
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
which we've talked about on the show before, there's no obvious main character.

191
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Maybe Brad Pitt would be the main character,

192
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
but there are many different actors and actresses who have smaller roles.

193
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,000
They're very important as well.

194
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
They're very important.

195
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:24,000
And one of these people actually won the award for best supporting actor this year.

196
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:30,000
That's right, and that person is an Austrian man by the name of Christoph Waltz.

197
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Waltz, he's amazing.

198
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
If you've ever seen this movie, you know exactly who he is.

199
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
He plays a Nazi, and every time he's on the screen,

200
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
he's the only thing you can look at.

201
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
He's so charismatic.

202
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
I think he was the Colonel Hans Landa,

203
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:51,000
and he was a very unique character in this movie,

204
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
very charismatic, but at the same time, he's the villain.

205
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
So it makes it even more intriguing because you always expect the villain

206
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
to be ugly or mean or to be like the beast, right?

207
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
But in this case, he's more of a nice guy, but he's the villain.

208
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,000
It's kind of strange.

209
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
He's very nice, but he's also evil, so it's really difficult.

210
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Very good acting on his behalf.

211
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Very good.

212
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,000
And best actress now, best supporting actress,

213
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:24,000
went also to the movie Precious, this girl named Monique.

214
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Monique.

215
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
So Monique has a talk show in America.

216
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:32,000
She's pretty well known, but not well known as a dramatic actress.

217
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
And so I'm really happy she won this.

218
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
The role, again, she was kind of a horrible woman in this movie.

219
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Very difficult, very mean, but sad too.

220
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:47,000
And I'm really glad she won this award because she was wonderful in that movie.

221
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Wow, very good.

222
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
So those were best supporting actors and actresses now.

223
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Moving on to the better ones.

224
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:58,000
Best actor went to Jeff Bridges in the movie Crazy Heart.

225
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,000
That's right.

226
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,000
I actually haven't seen this movie yet.

227
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
I haven't seen this movie either.

228
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
I bought it and I haven't had time to watch it.

229
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,000
I'm really looking forward to it.

230
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
I love Jeff Bridges, the dude.

231
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
I can't really remember him from any other movie.

232
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
He's famous for a lot of different movies,

233
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
but one of his most famous was The Big Lebowski,

234
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:23,000
where he's this main character who's just this lazy, weird guy who plays bowls.

235
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
But, yeah, Jeff Bridges is a wonderful actor

236
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
and I'm very happy for him that he won this.

237
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Yeah, very good.

238
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
I think it's his first Oscar, right?

239
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
I think so, yeah, I'm not sure.

240
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Okay, and moving on to best actress.

241
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:37,000
This was an interesting surprise,

242
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
and I'm actually really happy with the result.

243
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
Best actress went to Sandra Bullock for The Blind Side.

244
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
I loved this movie.

245
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
It's a feel-good movie.

246
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
It makes you just happy, you know?

247
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
And so few movies do that,

248
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and I've read a lot of negativity on the Internet.

249
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
You know, people who, unlike you, are angry that she won.

250
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,000
Yeah, a lot of them.

251
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:00,000
How could she dare win?

